[91m│                                                                              │[0m
[91m│ # V8 Sandbox - Readme                                                        │[0m
[91m│                                                                              │[0m
[91m│ A low-overhead, in-process sandbox for V8.                                   │[0m
[91m│                                                                              │[0m
[91m│ The sandbox limits the impact of typical V8 vulnerabilities by restricting   │[0m
[91m│ the                                                                          │[0m
[91m│ code executed by V8 to a subset of the process' virtual address space ("the  │[0m
[91m│ sandbox"), thereby isolating it from the rest of the process. This works     │[0m
[91m│ purely                                                                       │[0m
[91m│ in software (with options for hardware support, see the respective design    │[0m
[91m│ document linked below) by effectively converting raw pointers either into    │[0m
[91m│ offsets from the base of the sandbox or into indices into out-of-sandbox     │[0m
[91m│ pointer tables. In principle, these mechanisms are very similar to the       │[0m
[91m│ userland/kernel separation used by modern operating systems (e.g. the unix   │[0m
[91m│ file                                                                         │[0m
[91m│ descriptor table).                                                           │[0m
[91m│                                                                              │[0m
[91m│ The sandbox assumes that an attacker can arbitrarily and concurrently modify │[0m
[91m│ any memory inside the sandbox address space as this primitive can be         │[0m
[91m│ constructed from typical V8 vulnerabilities. Further, it is assumed that an  │[0m
[91m│ attacker will be able to read memory outside of the sandbox, for example     │[0m
[91m│ through hardware side channels. The sandbox then aims to protect the rest of │[0m
[91m│ the process from such an attacker. As such, any corruption of memory outside │[0m
[91m│ of                                                                           │[0m
[91m│ the sandbox address space is considered a sandbox violation.                 │[0m
[91m│                                                                              │[0m
[91m│ ## Usage                                                                     │[0m
[91m│                                                                              │[0m
[91m│ To enable the sandbox, simply use `v8_enable_sandbox = true` in the gn args. │[0m
[91m│ The sandbox is only available in 64-bit configurations as it requires large  │[0m
[91m│ amounts of virtual address space. The sandbox is enabled by default on       │[0m
[91m│ supported configurations.                                                    │[0m
[91m│                                                                              │[0m
[91m│ ## Testing                                                                   │[0m
[91m│                                                                              │[0m
[91m│ The sandbox is designed to be testable, both manually and automatically.     │[0m
[91m│                                                                              │[0m
[91m│ To use a "sandbox testing" configurations, two steps are required:           │[0m
[91m│                                                                              │[0m
[91m│ 1. V8 needs to be build with `v8_enable_memory_corruption_api = true`. This  │[0m
[91m│ will, when sandbox testing mode is enabled, expose a JavaScript `Sandbox`    │[0m
[91m│ object through which memory inside the sandbox can be arbitrarily modified.  │[0m
[91m│ This API effectively emulates common exploit primitives that can be          │[0m
[91m│ constructed from a typical V8 vulnerability.                                 │[0m
[91m│ 2. The sandbox testing mode needs to be enabled at runtime via either        │[0m
[91m│ `--sandbox-testing` or `--sandbox-fuzzing`. This will expose the memory      │[0m
[91m│ corruption API to JavaScript code and enable the sandbox crash filter, which │[0m
[91m│ will filter out uninteresting (for the sandbox) crashes such as access       │[0m
[91m│ violations inside the sandbox or other unexploitable crashes.                │[0m
[91m│ Note that the sandbox crash filter is currently only available on Linux.     │[0m
[91m│                                                                              │[0m
[91m│ The following example demonstrates these two parts:                          │[0m
[91m│                                                                              │[0m
[91m│ ```JavaScript                                                                │[0m
[91m│ // Create a DataView that can read and write inside the sandbox.             │[0m
[91m│ let memory = new DataView(new Sandbox.MemoryView(0, 0x100000000));           │[0m
[91m│                                                                              │[0m
[91m│ // Create an object to corrupt and obtain its address inside the sandbox.    │[0m
[91m│ let corruptMe = {};                                                          │[0m
[91m│ let addr = Sandbox.getAddressOf(corruptMe);                                  │[0m
[91m│                                                                              │[0m
[91m│ // Corrupt the object.                                                       │[0m
[91m│ memory.setUint32(addr, 0x41414141, true);                                    │[0m
[91m│ memory.setUint32(addr + 4, 0x41414141, true);                                │[0m
[91m│ memory.setUint32(addr + 8, 0x41414141, true);                                │[0m
[91m│                                                                              │[0m
[91m│ // Trigger a crash. The sandbox crash filter will catch it and determine     │[0m
[91m│ that                                                                         │[0m
[91m│ // this is _not_ a sandbox violation as it crashes inside the sandbox.       │[0m
[91m│ corruptMe.crash();                                                           │[0m
[91m│                                                                              │[0m
[91m│ // The process will now terminate cleanly with a message such as:            │[0m
[91m│ // "Caught harmless memory access violation (inside sandbox address space).  │[0m
[91m│ Exiting process..."                                                          │[0m
[91m│ ```                                                                          │[0m
[91m│                                                                              │[0m
[91m│ The --sandbox-testing and --sandbox-fuzzing flags are mostly identical.      │[0m
[91m│ However, with --sandbox-testing, the process will terminate with exit status │[0m
[91m│ zero when detecting a harmless crash. As such, tests will be treated as      │[0m
[91m│ passing                                                                      │[0m
[91m│ if they crash safely such as for example through a CHECK failure. With       │[0m
[91m│ --sandbox-fuzzing on the other hand, the process instead exits with a        │[0m
[91m│ non-zero                                                                     │[0m
[91m│ status so that fuzzers can determine that the execution terminated           │[0m
[91m│ prematurely.                                                                 │[0m
[91m│ Furthermore, the --sandbox-fuzzing requires the memory corruption API to be  │[0m
[91m│ compiled in while --sandbox-testing can be enabled on any (sandbox-enabled)  │[0m
[91m│ build. As such, --sandbox-fuzzing should generally be used for fuzzers while │[0m
[91m│ --sandbox-testing should be used for most other purposes.                    │[0m
[91m│                                                                              │[0m
[91m│ Note that both --sandbox-testing and --sandbox-fuzzing will also report      │[0m
[91m│ _reads_                                                                      │[0m
[91m│ outside the sandbox as "sandbox violations". This is not technically true as │[0m
[91m│ the sandbox only attempts to prevent _writes_. However it is both difficult  │[0m
[91m│ and                                                                          │[0m
[91m│ undesirable to filter out reads, for example because in some cases, the      │[0m
[91m│ underlying issue may also allow an attacker to perform a write instead.      │[0m
[91m│                                                                              │[0m
[91m│ ## Resources                                                                 │[0m
[91m│                                                                              │[0m
[91m│ The following list contains further resources about the sandbox, in          │[0m
[91m│ particular                                                                   │[0m
[91m│ various design documents related to it.                                      │[0m
[91m│                                                                              │[0m
[91m│ * [Sandbox Blog Post](https://v8.dev/blog/sandbox):                          │[0m
[91m│ Discusses the motivation behind the sandbox and its goals while also briefly │[0m
[91m│ covering the high-level design, performance characteristics, and testability │[0m
[91m│ aspects of the sandbox.                                                      │[0m
[91m│ * [High-Level Design                                                         │[0m
[91m│ Document](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1FM4fQmIhEqPG8uGp5o9A-mnPB5BOeS │[0m
[91m│ cZYpkHjo0KKA8/edit?usp=sharing):                                             │[0m
[91m│ Discusses the attacker model, goal, and basic design of the sandbox, while   │[0m
[91m│ linking to the more specific design documents below where appropriate.       │[0m
[91m│ * [Sandbox Address                                                           │[0m
[91m│ Space](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1PM4Zqmlt8ac5O8UNQfY7fOsem-6MhbsB- │[0m
[91m│ vjFI-9XK6w/edit?usp=sharing):                                                │[0m
[91m│ Contains additional details about the address space reservation backing the  │[0m
[91m│ sandbox and how it is allocated.                                             │[0m
[91m│ * [Sandboxed                                                                 │[0m
[91m│ Pointers](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1HSap8-J3HcrZvT7-5NsbYWcjfc0BVo │[0m
[91m│ ops5TDHZNsnko/edit?usp=sharing):                                             │[0m
[91m│ Discusses the design of sandboxed pointers, which are pointers that are      │[0m
[91m│ always guaranteed to point into the sandbox and are for example used to      │[0m
[91m│ reference ArrayBuffer backing stores.                                        │[0m
[91m│ * [External Pointer                                                          │[0m
[91m│ Table](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1V3sxltuFjjhp_6grGHgfqZNK57qfzGzme │[0m
[91m│ 0QTk0IXDHk/edit?usp=sharing):                                                │[0m
[91m│ The external pointer table is used to securely reference non-V8 ("external") │[0m
[91m│ objects, for example objects owned by the Embedder, from inside the sandbox. │[0m
[91m│ This document discusses its design in more detail.                           │[0m
[91m│ * [Code Pointer                                                              │[0m
[91m│ Sandboxing](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1CPs5PutbnmI-c5g7e_Td9CNGh5Bv │[0m
[91m│ pLleKCqUnqmD82k/edit?usp=sharing):                                           │[0m
[91m│ Similar to external objects, pointers to executable machine code also needs  │[0m
[91m│ to be protected. This is done with the help of a dedicated code pointer      │[0m
[91m│ table, which is discussed in this document.                                  │[0m
[91m│ * [Trusted                                                                   │[0m
[91m│ Space](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1IrvzL4uX_Zv0k2Iakdp_q_z33bj-qlYF5 │[0m
[91m│ IesGpXW0fM/edit?usp=sharing):                                                │[0m
[91m│ Certain V8 objects are considered trusted and must not be corrupted by an    │[0m
[91m│ attacker. Examples of such objects include bytecode containers and metadata  │[0m
[91m│ for JIT-generated code. These objects are protected by allocating them       │[0m
[91m│ outside of the sandbox in the trusted heap space, then referencing them      │[0m
[91m│ through another pointer table indirection to ensure memory safe access. This │[0m
[91m│ document discusses both of these mechanisms.                                 │[0m
[91m│ * [Hardware                                                                  │[0m
[91m│ Support](https://docs.google.com/document/d/12MsaG6BYRB-jQWNkZiuM3bY8X2B2cAs │[0m
[91m│ CMLLdgErvK4c/edit?usp=sharing):                                              │[0m
[91m│ Instead of the purely software-based sandbox described by the preceeding     │[0m
[91m│ documents, the sandbox could also be implemented (or augmented) with special │[0m
[91m│ hardware support. This document discusses various options for how that may   │[0m
[91m│ work in practice, what the implications would be, and which requirements the │[0m
[91m│ hardware would have to fulfill.                                              │[0m
[91m│ * [Bytecode                                                                  │[0m
[91m│ Verification](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1UUooVKUvf1zDobG34VDVuLsjoK │[0m
[91m│ Zd-CeSuhvBcLysc7U/edit?usp=sharing):                                         │[0m
[91m│ A simple bytecode verifier that ensures bytecode is safe to execute.         │[0m
[91m│                                                                              │[0m
